export const CHILD_SAFETY_STANDARDS_HTML: string = "<h1>Pool \u2014 Child Safety Standards</h1>\n<p><strong>Effective date:</strong> 18 September 2026\n<strong>Last updated:</strong> 18 September 2026</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Published at <code>https://poolapp.co/child-safety</code>.</strong> This file is the source of\ntruth; the site page is generated from it.</p>\n<p>These are Pool&#39;s published standards against child sexual abuse and\nexploitation (CSAE), required of apps in Google Play&#39;s Social category under\nthe <a href=\"https://support.google.com/googleplay/android-developer/answer/14747720\">Child Safety Standards policy</a>.\nEvery claim below describes the product as actually built.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h2>1. Our position</h2>\n<p>Child sexual abuse and exploitation is <strong>absolutely prohibited on Pool</strong>. This\nincludes child sexual abuse material (CSAM), sexualization of minors, grooming,\nsextortion, trafficking, and any attempt to use Pool to arrange, promote or\nfacilitate harm to a child.</p>\n<p>There is no warning, no strike system and no appeal for this category. An\naccount used for CSAE is removed, and confirmed CSAM is reported to the\nauthorities named in \u00a76.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>2. Pool is an adults-only service</h2>\n<p><strong>You must be 18 or older to hold a Pool account.</strong> This is not a checkbox \u2014 the\nage floor is enforced in three independent places:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>In the app</strong>, which refuses an under-18 date of birth at signup.</li>\n<li><strong>On our servers</strong>, which re-check the date of birth on every profile write\nrather than trusting the app.</li>\n<li><strong>In the database itself</strong>, where a constraint <em>rejects</em> an under-age date of\nbirth outright. It refuses the write rather than silently discarding the\ndate, so an under-age account cannot be created by a bug, an out-of-date app\nversion, or a direct API call.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>We do not knowingly collect information from anyone under 18. If you believe a\nminor holds an account, contact us (\u00a77) and we will remove it.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>3. What can be shared on Pool</h2>\n<p>Pool is a group-spending app, not a social network. Understanding its surface is\npart of understanding our risk:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>There is no public content feed</strong>, no comments on strangers&#39; content, and no\nvideo or voice.</li>\n<li><strong>Profiles are private by default.</strong> You appear in Discover only if you\nexplicitly turn discoverability on.</li>\n<li><strong>User-provided content is limited to</strong>: a display name and username, a short\nbio, interests and occupation, a profile photo, and pool names, descriptions,\nhouse rules and cover images.</li>\n<li><strong>In-app messaging is disabled in the current release.</strong> When it is enabled,\nit is one-to-one or within a pool you have joined \u2014 not open broadcast \u2014 and\nevery message is subject to the reporting and moderation described below.</li>\n<li><strong>Receipt photos are deleted immediately after they are read</strong>, and are never\nshown to another user.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pool contains no advertising and no third-party ad SDKs.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>4. Prevention</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The 18+ gate above</strong>, which is the primary control.</li>\n<li><strong>No anonymous access.</strong> Every account is tied to a verified email address.\nThere are no throwaway or guest accounts.</li>\n<li><strong>Discovery is opt-in</strong>, so an account is not exposed to strangers by default.</li>\n<li><strong>Automated screening of pool names, descriptions, tags and house rules</strong>\nagainst a prohibited-terms list, applied before the content is visible. The\nmatcher normalizes character substitutions (&quot;leetspeak&quot;) and strips invisible\ncharacters first, so the obvious evasions do not work, and it runs on our\nservers rather than in the app \u2014 the app&#39;s copy is only there to give faster\nfeedback, and cannot be bypassed to reach the database.</li>\n<li><strong>Blocking</strong>, which removes both people from each other&#39;s Discover results and\nrefuses contact in both directions.</li>\n<li><strong>Rate limits and abuse controls</strong> on account creation and messaging.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h2>5. Reporting \u2014 how to tell us</h2>\n<p><strong>In the app.</strong> Any user or piece of content can be reported from the app: open\nthe profile or content, choose <strong>Report</strong>, and select a reason. Reports reach our\nmoderation queue directly. <strong>The report and block controls remain available even\nwhen messaging is switched off</strong> \u2014 we deliberately do not tie our safety surface\nto a feature flag.</p>\n<p><strong>By email.</strong> Anyone \u2014 including people who do not use Pool \u2014 may report child\nsafety concerns to <strong><a href=\"mailto:support@poolapp.co\">support@poolapp.co</a></strong>. You do\nnot need an account to contact us.</p>\n<p><strong>Do not forward suspected CSAM to us.</strong> Possessing or transmitting it is a\ncrime. Report the account, username or pool, and we will retrieve what we need\nfrom our own systems.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>6. How we respond</h2>\n<p>When we receive a child safety report:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CSAE reports are prioritized above every other report type</strong> and are\nactioned ahead of the rest of the moderation queue.</li>\n<li>We <strong>preserve the reported content and surrounding context</strong> as evidence\nbefore removing it, so an investigation is not destroyed by the takedown.</li>\n<li>We <strong>remove the content and terminate the account</strong>. Termination is permanent.</li>\n<li>Where we identify apparent CSAM, we <strong>report it to the National Center for\nMissing &amp; Exploited Children (NCMEC)</strong> through the CyberTipline, and to law\nenforcement where required.</li>\n<li>We <strong>preserve records</strong> as required by law and cooperate with lawful requests\nfrom law enforcement.</li>\n<li>We may <strong>notify Google Play</strong> where the policy requires it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We do not publish a fixed response-time guarantee here, because we will not\npublish a commitment we cannot keep. What we commit to is the <strong>priority</strong>: child\nsafety reports go to the front of the queue, ahead of everything else.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>7. Point of contact</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Report a child safety concern:</strong>\n<strong><a href=\"mailto:support@poolapp.co\">support@poolapp.co</a></strong></li>\n<li><strong>Designated child safety point of contact for regulators and platforms\n(including the contact registered with Google Play):</strong>\n<strong><a href=\"mailto:admin@poolapp.co\">admin@poolapp.co</a></strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Pool Labs Inc. \u00b7 St. Louis, Missouri, USA</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>8. Compliance</h2>\n<p>Pool complies with applicable child safety laws in the jurisdictions where it\noperates, including US federal reporting obligations for apparent CSAM, and with\nGoogle Play&#39;s Child Safety Standards policy and Apple&#39;s App Store Review\nGuidelines on objectionable content.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>9. Changes</h2>\n<p>We will post changes here and update the &quot;Last updated&quot; date. Our prohibition on\nCSAE is not subject to change.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><em>See also: <a href=\"https://poolapp.co/privacy\">Privacy Policy</a> \u00b7\n<a href=\"https://poolapp.co/terms\">Terms of Service</a></em></p>\n";
